        "thinking": "Found it! `server/src/routes/assets.ts` imports `jsdom` directly, and this route is also imported by `api.ts` in the CF worker:\n```ts\nimport { assetRoutes } from \"../../../../server/src/routes/assets.js\";\n```\n\nThis causes `jsdom` (and thus `undici@7` and `node:sqlite`) to be bundled.\n\nNow, there are a few approaches to fix this:\n\n1. **Lazy import**: Make `jsdom` import lazy/dynamic in `assets.ts` so it's only pulled in when used\n\n\n2. **Mark as external**: Tell esbuild to mark `jsdom` and its deps as external\n3. **Refactor**: Move the jsdom-dependent code to a separate file and don't import it in CF\n\nFor the CF worker, the cleanest solution is either lazy-loading jsdom in assets.ts so it only gets imported when actually needed, or marking jsdom and node:sqlite as external in the wrangler config. Since I need to handle both the embedded-postgres issue and the jsdom bundling problem...\n\nFor embedded-postgres, I should fix the import in api.ts to pull directly from the schema path instead of the full package. For jsdom, lazy-loading feels cleaner than adding wrangler config changes. Let me check what api.ts is actually importing... I see the real issue now—`assets.ts` is statically imported at the top of `api.ts`, so esbuild bundles it regardless of whether jsdom is actually used, pulling in that dependency for the CF worker. Making jsdom a lazy import within `assets.ts` would solve this without removing the SVG sanitization feature entirely. For the broader fix, I should change the dynamic imports in `api.ts` to import directly from the schema instead of re-exporting through the package.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
